1
00:00:00,860 --> 00:00:01,860
A castle burns.

2
00:00:01,940 --> 00:00:08,550
An old man walks out of it: no sword,
no men, no one at his side.

3
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:11,650
All he had built in a lifetime,

4
00:00:11,650 --> 00:00:15,010
he had handed over to his sons
himself, just days before.

5
00:00:15,580 --> 00:00:17,330
So why is he alone now?

6
00:00:17,410 --> 00:00:21,940
And the real question is this:
does what you do come back to you one day?

7
00:00:23,320 --> 00:00:25,130
This is Akira Kurosawa's Ran.

8
00:00:25,210 --> 00:00:27,130
In Japanese, it means chaos.

9
00:00:27,210 --> 00:00:30,120
The year is 1985.

10
00:00:30,975 --> 00:00:35,755
But the story itself is much older:
Shakespeare's King Lear.

11
00:00:36,305 --> 00:00:38,925
Kurosawa added just one thing to it.

12
00:00:39,005 --> 00:00:41,275
Something Shakespeare never gave him.

13
00:00:42,975 --> 00:00:44,795
Hidetora is an old warlord.

14
00:00:44,875 --> 00:00:48,575
He has spent his whole life
waging war to build a realm.

15
00:00:49,205 --> 00:00:53,265
Returning from a hunt, he announces
his decision: now he will rest.

16
00:00:53,345 --> 00:00:58,335
He will divide the land among his three
sons, but keep his title and his honor.

17
00:00:59,165 --> 00:01:01,215
Then he gives each son an arrow.

18
00:01:01,295 --> 00:01:03,845
A single arrow breaks easily.

19
00:01:03,925 --> 00:01:05,945
Three bound together won't break.

20
00:01:06,945 --> 00:01:11,545
This lesson comes from an old Japanese
legend: the advice a 16th-century

21
00:01:11,545 --> 00:01:15,555
lord, Mōri Motonari,
gave to his sons.

22
00:01:16,435 --> 00:01:21,645
Kurosawa found himself wondering:
what if those three sons weren't good?

23
00:01:22,625 --> 00:01:27,255
The youngest son, Saburo, breaks
all three arrows across his knee.

24
00:01:27,335 --> 00:01:32,065
He says his brothers will never
be able to share this land.

25
00:01:32,460 --> 00:01:34,225
Hidetora banishes him.

26
00:01:34,305 --> 00:01:38,565
Just as Lear banishes Cordelia,
the daughter who refused to flatter him.

27
00:01:38,655 --> 00:01:42,875
That day, Lear tells his daughter:
“Nothing will come of nothing.”

28
00:01:44,085 --> 00:01:48,055
Shakespeare has three daughters;
Kurosawa, three sons.

29
00:01:48,135 --> 00:01:50,355
But the real difference is elsewhere.

30
00:01:52,035 --> 00:01:54,995
Hidetora first goes to
eldest son Taro's castle.

31
00:01:55,075 --> 00:01:59,305
There he is met by Taro's wife:
Kaede.

32
00:01:59,495 --> 00:02:02,465
Taro defies his father.
It's Kaede who goads him.

33
00:02:02,545 --> 00:02:06,575
Because Kaede has a past.

34
00:02:06,655 --> 00:02:09,595
Years ago Hidetora put
her family to the sword.

35
00:02:09,675 --> 00:02:11,969
Now she was the wife of his son.

36
00:02:12,165 --> 00:02:17,095
In this story, Hidetora has wounded
three people and left them alive.

37
00:02:17,175 --> 00:02:19,525
One of them will become a monster.

38
00:02:20,485 --> 00:02:26,295
That was what Kurosawa added:
a past.

39
00:02:27,025 --> 00:02:31,075
In Kurosawa's view, Shakespeare
gives his heroes no past.

40
00:02:31,155 --> 00:02:33,225
In Ran, the king has a past.

41
00:02:33,305 --> 00:02:35,065
And that past is bloody.

42
00:02:35,975 --> 00:02:37,685
Taro shuts the gate.

43
00:02:37,765 --> 00:02:39,785
Then so does Jiro, the middle son.

44
00:02:40,315 --> 00:02:42,445
Lear, too, went from door to door.

45
00:02:42,525 --> 00:02:47,915
“How sharper than a serpent’s tooth it is
To have a thankless child!”

46
00:02:49,665 --> 00:02:54,605
Hidetora takes refuge in the one place
left: exiled Saburo's empty castle.

47
00:02:55,205 --> 00:02:59,185
But the armies of Taro and Jiro

48
00:02:59,185 --> 00:03:01,975
besiege the castle.

49
00:03:02,055 --> 00:03:03,585
His men fall one by one.

50
00:03:03,665 --> 00:03:07,385
The castle burns. Hidetora reaches
for his sword; it is broken.

51
00:03:08,055 --> 00:03:11,555
Then the moment: the old man
walks down from the flames.

52
00:03:11,635 --> 00:03:13,365
The soldiers step aside.

53
00:03:13,445 --> 00:03:14,875
No one touches him.

54
00:03:16,105 --> 00:03:17,695
Why is he alone?

55
00:03:17,775 --> 00:03:20,355
Because he built it all by the sword.

56
00:03:20,515 --> 00:03:22,945
Now that sword had turned on him.

57
00:03:24,615 --> 00:03:27,215
Kurosawa had this castle built for real

58
00:03:27,215 --> 00:03:29,805
at the foot of Mount Fuji,
then burned it.

59
00:03:29,885 --> 00:03:33,885
The scene was shot in a single take;
there was no second chance.

60
00:03:35,745 --> 00:03:39,955
Long before shooting it, Kurosawa
had already painted this scene.

61
00:03:40,665 --> 00:03:43,615
He wrote the screenplay
in the mid-seventies.

62
00:03:43,715 --> 00:03:47,755
But no studio would fund
such an expensive film.

63
00:03:48,445 --> 00:03:51,845
While waiting, Kurosawa painted
the film frame by frame.

64
00:03:51,995 --> 00:03:53,415
Hundreds of paintings.

65
00:03:54,105 --> 00:03:58,275
The money came only years later,
from a French producer.

66
00:03:58,355 --> 00:04:03,895
When shooting began, Kurosawa was past
seventy, and his eyes were failing badly.

67
00:04:04,685 --> 00:04:07,735
The crew built the scenes
from those paintings.

68
00:04:08,435 --> 00:04:12,745
I won't show those paintings here;
they all still belong to Kurosawa.

69
00:04:12,825 --> 00:04:15,765
We'll rebuild them with our own brush.

70
00:04:17,490 --> 00:04:19,890
At this point, Lear too goes mad.

71
00:04:19,970 --> 00:04:24,940
In the middle of the storm, he cries:
“Blow, winds, and crack your cheeks!”

72
00:04:25,410 --> 00:04:29,240
And then his most famous line:
“I am a man

73
00:04:29,240 --> 00:04:32,200
More sinn’d against than sinning.”

74
00:04:33,062 --> 00:04:34,180
True for Lear.

75
00:04:34,260 --> 00:04:36,180
Not for Hidetora.

76
00:04:37,440 --> 00:04:43,400
This acting can put viewers off:
exaggerated, heavy, a face like a mask.

77
00:04:44,010 --> 00:04:45,670
That is no accident.

78
00:04:45,750 --> 00:04:48,830
It echoes Nō theater,
over six centuries old:

79
00:04:48,830 --> 00:04:52,260
the face is a mask; the body
carries the emotion.

80
00:04:54,040 --> 00:04:58,000
Out of his mind, Hidetora
wanders among the ruins.

81
00:04:58,080 --> 00:05:03,500
One night, in the hut where he shelters,
a young man meets him: Tsurumaru.

82
00:05:04,350 --> 00:05:09,960
Hidetora had killed Tsurumaru's family
and had his eyes put out.

83
00:05:10,760 --> 00:05:13,250
Tsurumaru plays the flute for him.

84
00:05:13,330 --> 00:05:15,790
Hidetora cannot bear the melody.

85
00:05:17,450 --> 00:05:19,800
Now the real war isn't with his sons.

86
00:05:19,880 --> 00:05:21,640
It's with his own conscience.

87
00:05:23,160 --> 00:05:26,370
Tsurumaru's sister Sue
had suffered the same pain.

88
00:05:26,450 --> 00:05:32,690
Her family destroyed by Hidetora's sword,
she was given to Jiro as a bride.

89
00:05:33,430 --> 00:05:34,920
But Sue didn't hate him.

90
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:37,190
She forgave Hidetora.

91
00:05:38,030 --> 00:05:40,970
Kaede, though, forgave nothing.

92
00:05:42,850 --> 00:05:45,810
Saburo, in exile, comes
looking for his father.

93
00:05:45,890 --> 00:05:49,990
Seeing him, Hidetora comes to his senses
and asks his forgiveness.

94
00:05:50,910 --> 00:05:54,010
It is the same in Shakespeare:
Cordelia returns,

95
00:05:54,010 --> 00:05:56,680
and the old king wants
to kneel before her.

96
00:05:57,380 --> 00:05:59,590
For a moment, all seems set right.

97
00:06:00,210 --> 00:06:03,830
But a shot fired from afar
strikes Saburo.

98
00:06:03,910 --> 00:06:06,380
Hidetora dies over his son's body.

99
00:06:07,970 --> 00:06:10,510
In Shakespeare, Cordelia dies too.

100
00:06:10,590 --> 00:06:14,320
Lear carries her in his arms
and says “never” five times.

101
00:06:15,430 --> 00:06:17,720
Kaede's revenge is complete too.

102
00:06:17,800 --> 00:06:19,390
Hidetora's house falls.

103
00:06:19,470 --> 00:06:23,930
Kaede says she wanted this from the start,
and then she too dies.

104
00:06:24,970 --> 00:06:28,870
And Sue? That night she returned
for the flute her brother forgot.

105
00:06:28,950 --> 00:06:30,840
She never came back.

106
00:06:32,920 --> 00:06:38,850
The film's final shot: at the edge
of a ruined wall, blind Tsurumaru, alone.

107
00:06:39,590 --> 00:06:41,860
No flute. No sister.

108
00:06:41,940 --> 00:06:45,140
Even the picture his sister left
falls from his hands.

109
00:06:46,080 --> 00:06:47,570
There is no sound.

110
00:06:49,920 --> 00:06:52,250
Three people suffered the same pain.

111
00:06:52,330 --> 00:06:54,010
Kaede burned it all down.

112
00:06:54,090 --> 00:06:57,910
Sue forgave.
Tsurumaru was left in darkness.

113
00:06:58,304 --> 00:06:59,480
Kaede: a monster.

114
00:06:59,560 --> 00:07:01,120
But Hidetora made her one.

115
00:07:01,200 --> 00:07:04,200
Yet Sue, with the same wound,
chose another path.

116
00:07:04,720 --> 00:07:08,560
Kurosawa finished this film
at seventy-five.

117
00:07:08,830 --> 00:07:12,060
During the shoot, he lost his wife

118
00:07:12,060 --> 00:07:16,050
of thirty-nine years, Yōko;
he stopped work for just one day.

119
00:07:17,019 --> 00:07:19,960
The past he gave Lear
leaves us a question too.

120
00:07:20,100 --> 00:07:23,650
Does what you do come back to you one day?

121
00:07:23,730 --> 00:07:26,120
For Hidetora, yes.

122
00:07:26,850 --> 00:07:32,810
But the real question is the other one:
what do you do with what was done to you?

123
00:07:34,278 --> 00:07:36,690
We bring such questions from the past.

124
00:07:36,770 --> 00:07:40,340
If you want to continue this journey,
subscribe.

125
00:07:41,090 --> 00:07:46,880
The story of a man who pushes his rock
again each day: Sisyphus, now on screen.
